WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:07.765
&gt;&gt; Hello, everyone.
Good afternoon.

00:00:07.766 --> 00:00:09.360
Welcome to the AI

00:00:09.361 --> 00:00:11.275
Infrastructure and
the two-hour session.

00:00:11.276 --> 00:00:13.010
This session we will
have two talks.

00:00:13.011 --> 00:00:17.090
One is about backend optimizer

00:00:17.091 --> 00:00:18.435
for deep learning computation.

00:00:18.436 --> 00:00:21.100
Another one is for AI print form.

00:00:21.101 --> 00:00:24.435
So, I'm Jilong Xue from
Microsoft Research, Asia.

00:00:24.436 --> 00:00:26.535
So, I will start first talk.

00:00:26.536 --> 00:00:29.940
It's glad to introduce
our recent project

00:00:29.941 --> 00:00:31.230
called the Wolong: A Backend

00:00:31.231 --> 00:00:33.340
Optimizer for
Deep Learning Computation.

00:00:33.341 --> 00:00:38.075
In recent years,
deep learning has gained

00:00:38.076 --> 00:00:39.680
many success in the areas such

00:00:39.681 --> 00:00:43.460
as vision and speech
and NLP and so on.

00:00:43.461 --> 00:00:44.960
Along with this success,

00:00:44.961 --> 00:00:48.535
there are many
emerging innovations.

00:00:48.536 --> 00:00:51.125
In one side is
a deep learning side

00:00:51.126 --> 00:00:53.000
are continuously inventing

00:00:53.001 --> 00:00:55.615
new models and workload patterns.

00:00:55.616 --> 00:00:59.480
For example, beyond the RNN
and CNN, the typical models,

00:00:59.481 --> 00:01:03.125
there are also new workload
patterns like GAN,

00:01:03.126 --> 00:01:07.465
reinforcement learning and
graph neural networks.

00:01:07.466 --> 00:01:08.940
So, in other sides of

00:01:08.941 --> 00:01:11.100
the hardware vendors
are actually inventing

00:01:11.101 --> 00:01:15.425
new accelerators for
speed up the computation.

00:01:15.426 --> 00:01:19.410
For example, beyond
the CPU, GPU and FPGA,

00:01:19.411 --> 00:01:26.060
there are some more and
more new basic accelerators

00:01:26.061 --> 00:01:28.490
and high-speed network links.

00:01:28.491 --> 00:01:30.545
So, using this situations,

00:01:30.546 --> 00:01:32.780
there were deep learning
frameworks were proposed.

00:01:32.781 --> 00:01:36.775
For example, like
Tensorflow, NTK, Caffe2,

00:01:36.776 --> 00:01:39.625
for user to easily
write a DN program,

00:01:39.626 --> 00:01:42.775
and the computer on
different hardware devices.

00:01:42.776 --> 00:01:45.140
Typically, these
frameworks epsilon and

00:01:45.141 --> 00:01:48.210
deep learning computation
as a dataflow graph,

00:01:48.211 --> 00:01:51.605
the node, it's work is to permit

00:01:51.606 --> 00:01:55.695
operators and the edge
represents the tensor data.

00:01:55.696 --> 00:01:58.410
So, using dataflow graph
as a programming IR,

00:01:58.411 --> 00:01:59.440
you do get efficient,

00:01:59.441 --> 00:02:02.315
expensive and
programming flexibility.

00:02:02.316 --> 00:02:04.760
However, it also brings
the challenge of how to

00:02:04.761 --> 00:02:09.580
effectively do graph computation
over different devices.

00:02:09.581 --> 00:02:11.870
So, we think a systematic way

00:02:11.871 --> 00:02:13.955
is to leverage our compiler and

00:02:13.956 --> 00:02:16.680
optimize our infrastructure to

00:02:16.681 --> 00:02:19.515
bridge the IR framework
and the hardware.

00:02:19.516 --> 00:02:21.320
This is because compilers can

00:02:21.321 --> 00:02:23.595
combine both the information from

00:02:23.596 --> 00:02:27.300
application pattern and
the hardware feature

00:02:27.301 --> 00:02:29.720
to make a best match.

00:02:29.721 --> 00:02:33.640
As it has a global view
of the dataflow graph,

00:02:33.641 --> 00:02:35.840
so the compiler and optimizer can

00:02:35.841 --> 00:02:39.350
also optimize for
distributed computation.

00:02:39.351 --> 00:02:41.255
Thus, such infrastructure is

00:02:41.256 --> 00:02:45.165
critical for both the training
and inference scenario.

00:02:45.166 --> 00:02:47.605
Based on this observation,

00:02:47.606 --> 00:02:50.840
we started our research
project one year ago.

00:02:50.841 --> 00:02:54.980
So, we proposed a system
called the Wolong,

00:02:54.981 --> 00:02:58.920
which is optimizer stack for
deep learning computation.

00:02:58.921 --> 00:03:01.010
The motivation of
Wolong is to adjust

00:03:01.011 --> 00:03:03.320
deep learning performance
organization problem

00:03:03.321 --> 00:03:04.830
in a systematic way.

00:03:04.831 --> 00:03:07.500
Specifically, we're
going to leverage

00:03:07.501 --> 00:03:10.895
the general graph organization,

00:03:10.896 --> 00:03:15.290
passes and the leveraging
the principle for software

00:03:15.291 --> 00:03:20.220
and hardware co-design and
just-in-time compiler.

00:03:20.221 --> 00:03:23.745
The right figure is
the architecture of Wolong.

00:03:23.746 --> 00:03:27.440
Wolong has three
optimizer module;

00:03:27.441 --> 00:03:29.840
the global optimizer,
local optimizer

00:03:29.841 --> 00:03:32.600
and tensor placement
and optimizer.

00:03:32.601 --> 00:03:35.255
So, the global optimizer has

00:03:35.256 --> 00:03:38.330
a global view of the computation
graph and conducts

00:03:38.331 --> 00:03:41.090
a distributed organization
through graph

00:03:41.091 --> 00:03:44.910
partitioning and leverage
of the hardware features.

00:03:44.911 --> 00:03:49.510
The local optimizer mainly
optimize the subgraph after

00:03:49.511 --> 00:03:50.630
the graph partitioning for

00:03:50.631 --> 00:03:54.070
a specific device level
using the JIT compiler,

00:03:54.071 --> 00:03:57.135
and the tensor placement
optimizer mainly optimize

00:03:57.136 --> 00:04:01.005
for tensor layout
and replacement.

00:04:01.006 --> 00:04:02.750
All these optimizations, Wolong

00:04:02.751 --> 00:04:05.625
designing automatic
and transparent way.

00:04:05.626 --> 00:04:08.680
So, Wolong generate target user,

00:04:08.681 --> 00:04:12.590
optimizes the communication and

00:04:12.591 --> 00:04:15.869
the execution and
the memory efficiencies.

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:20.925
So, first let's look at
a case of global optimizer.

00:04:20.926 --> 00:04:23.290
The subtitle is a fast
distributed deep

00:04:23.291 --> 00:04:26.515
learning computation over RDMA.

00:04:26.516 --> 00:04:28.570
As I introduced it before,

00:04:28.571 --> 00:04:30.260
a deep learning computation

00:04:30.261 --> 00:04:32.405
is modeled as a dataflow graph.

00:04:32.406 --> 00:04:35.480
To execute the data flow graph
in distributed servers.

00:04:35.481 --> 00:04:38.150
So, DL framework first
need to partition

00:04:38.151 --> 00:04:39.770
the graph and the dispatch

00:04:39.771 --> 00:04:42.315
each sub-graph to
corresponding servers.

00:04:42.316 --> 00:04:47.160
After partitioning, F0 user
end go across two servers.

00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:49.715
We need to insert a pair of

00:04:49.716 --> 00:04:51.360
standard and the receiving nodes.

00:04:51.361 --> 00:04:53.900
So, these parallel of
node are responsible for

00:04:53.901 --> 00:04:58.139
a real data transmission
during the computation.

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:02.405
Worth to mention,
this kind of mechanism is

00:05:02.406 --> 00:05:05.060
generally to express
both modal parallelism,

00:05:05.061 --> 00:05:06.570
the data-parallel pattern,

00:05:06.571 --> 00:05:08.090
which are a common way to scale

00:05:08.091 --> 00:05:13.325
deep learning computation
in distributed servers.

00:05:13.326 --> 00:05:15.085
In such a general framework,

00:05:15.086 --> 00:05:16.790
the communication has to leverage

00:05:16.791 --> 00:05:19.925
a general communication library.

00:05:19.926 --> 00:05:22.575
However, in most deep
learning scenarios,

00:05:22.576 --> 00:05:23.790
the tensor transmission in

00:05:23.791 --> 00:05:26.785
such a framework brings
significant overhead,

00:05:26.786 --> 00:05:30.620
which becomes the major
bottleneck for scalability.

00:05:30.621 --> 00:05:33.530
To demonstrate this, we use
a simple micro benchmark,

00:05:33.531 --> 00:05:35.750
we just send a tensor

00:05:35.751 --> 00:05:38.900
with different side from
one server to another server.

00:05:38.901 --> 00:05:42.290
This figure measures the latency

00:05:42.291 --> 00:05:46.500
of different tensor size.

00:05:46.501 --> 00:05:53.230
As we can see, you
measure the same network,

00:05:53.231 --> 00:05:56.630
there is up to 10x
latency gap between

00:05:56.631 --> 00:05:59.030
the general RPC library
and Nobel optimizer of

00:05:59.031 --> 00:06:02.520
the program which
carefully leverage RDMA.

00:06:02.590 --> 00:06:06.450
So, to understand
the overhead of RPC,

00:06:06.451 --> 00:06:08.770
let's go to a little
bit more details.

00:06:08.771 --> 00:06:11.030
We observed that
the major overhead of

00:06:11.031 --> 00:06:13.680
RPC is caused by memory copy,

00:06:13.681 --> 00:06:15.420
and it is inherently

00:06:15.421 --> 00:06:17.870
unavoidable for
such a general library.

00:06:17.871 --> 00:06:20.300
This is because the general
IPS is designed for

00:06:20.301 --> 00:06:22.670
dynamic data structure and

00:06:22.671 --> 00:06:24.095
it's a lack of knowledge about

00:06:24.096 --> 00:06:27.825
the actual data placement
and the message size.

00:06:27.826 --> 00:06:30.465
As illustrated in
the right figure,

00:06:30.466 --> 00:06:35.030
a message is sent
from sender has to be

00:06:35.031 --> 00:06:37.355
first copied from
application memory

00:06:37.356 --> 00:06:40.635
to the RPC managed memory,

00:06:40.636 --> 00:06:43.310
and then serialize
into a larger buffer

00:06:43.311 --> 00:06:46.315
to better leverage
the batch efficiency.

00:06:46.316 --> 00:06:49.280
When it comes to
memory, disorientation

00:06:49.281 --> 00:06:52.915
and memory copy also happened
in the receiver side.

00:06:52.916 --> 00:06:54.530
To adjust this overhand,

00:06:54.531 --> 00:06:56.760
we cannot leverage
such a general library.

00:06:56.761 --> 00:07:00.470
Instead, we should use
a right time optimizer,

00:07:00.471 --> 00:07:02.210
which can dynamically decide

00:07:02.211 --> 00:07:03.860
the memory placement according to

00:07:03.861 --> 00:07:06.080
the specific computation
workload and

00:07:06.081 --> 00:07:08.060
the net network topology to

00:07:08.061 --> 00:07:11.120
completely remove
the memory overhead.

00:07:12.830 --> 00:07:15.925
Just in our distributed
graph optimization,

00:07:15.926 --> 00:07:18.020
we combine both the properties
are computed in

00:07:18.021 --> 00:07:20.440
a graph and RDMA network feature.

00:07:20.441 --> 00:07:24.090
First, for the tensor structure,

00:07:24.091 --> 00:07:26.175
unlike the general messy type,

00:07:26.176 --> 00:07:28.650
the tensor data in
deep learning computation

00:07:28.651 --> 00:07:31.125
has several special properties.

00:07:31.126 --> 00:07:34.280
First, it consists of
a plain byte array with

00:07:34.281 --> 00:07:36.630
sufficiently large size often

00:07:36.631 --> 00:07:39.335
from several kilobytes
to megabytes.

00:07:39.336 --> 00:07:41.000
Due to this, we actually do

00:07:41.001 --> 00:07:42.605
not require
data serialization and

00:07:42.606 --> 00:07:46.055
deserialization in
this transformation.

00:07:46.056 --> 00:07:49.805
Due to excess pattern of
tensors already sequential,

00:07:49.806 --> 00:07:51.940
we also do not require
extra batching,

00:07:51.941 --> 00:07:54.119
but all this information

00:07:54.120 --> 00:07:56.510
can only be decided
in the right time.

00:07:56.511 --> 00:08:01.310
So, second, with RDMA
hardware and the protocol,

00:08:01.311 --> 00:08:04.100
we can manage both the local
and distributed memory

00:08:04.101 --> 00:08:05.735
in a unified view.

00:08:05.736 --> 00:08:07.520
We can do efficient
the memory copy

00:08:07.521 --> 00:08:09.380
between host memory through

00:08:09.381 --> 00:08:13.230
the one side RDMA and read
and write primitives,

00:08:13.231 --> 00:08:15.940
and efficient memory copy
between device memory

00:08:15.941 --> 00:08:20.960
to host memory with
GPU direct RDMA.

00:08:20.961 --> 00:08:24.490
This global graph optimizer for

00:08:24.491 --> 00:08:27.110
distributed computation
is a systematic way

00:08:27.111 --> 00:08:29.510
to address scalability
of the other work,

00:08:29.511 --> 00:08:32.030
this is because the first
global optimizer account

00:08:32.031 --> 00:08:34.130
has entire view of and

00:08:34.131 --> 00:08:36.170
the control of the
memory placement

00:08:36.171 --> 00:08:38.670
among the devices servers.

00:08:38.671 --> 00:08:42.295
Second, it is capable of making
global optimizer strategy

00:08:42.296 --> 00:08:46.860
for this tensor data placement
in the right time.

00:08:46.861 --> 00:08:49.255
So, based on these observations,

00:08:49.256 --> 00:08:52.070
we designed a new
communication mechanism in

00:08:52.071 --> 00:08:56.540
our optimizer for distributed
computation over RDMA.

00:08:56.541 --> 00:08:59.620
In this mechanism,
it has two phases;

00:08:59.621 --> 00:09:03.965
the graph analyzing phase and
the graph execution phase.

00:09:03.966 --> 00:09:06.710
First, before
the graph is executed,

00:09:06.711 --> 00:09:08.935
there is a graph analyzing phase.

00:09:08.936 --> 00:09:10.580
In the sender side,

00:09:10.581 --> 00:09:12.290
our optimizer first detect

00:09:12.291 --> 00:09:15.235
physical memory address
of the sending tensor,

00:09:15.236 --> 00:09:17.840
and it tells the information
to the tensor manager

00:09:17.841 --> 00:09:20.770
to reallocate it as
the RDMA compatible memory.

00:09:20.771 --> 00:09:22.940
So, this can dynamically

00:09:22.941 --> 00:09:25.420
avoid the memory copy
in the sender side.

00:09:25.421 --> 00:09:27.230
Similarly, in the receiver side,

00:09:27.231 --> 00:09:28.945
as your graph is analyzing,

00:09:28.946 --> 00:09:31.565
we can know the receiving tensor
side beforehand.

00:09:31.566 --> 00:09:32.880
So, we can pre-allocate

00:09:32.881 --> 00:09:36.435
the receiving tensor as
RDMA compatible memory,

00:09:36.436 --> 00:09:40.215
to avoid the receiver side
in the memory copy overhead.

00:09:40.216 --> 00:09:43.235
So, after the graph
analyzing phase,

00:09:43.236 --> 00:09:45.370
in the execution phase,

00:09:45.371 --> 00:09:47.320
when the sender know

00:09:47.321 --> 00:09:49.110
there is ready to send
and source tensor,

00:09:49.111 --> 00:09:53.420
it can just issue
a one-sided RDMA right command.

00:09:53.421 --> 00:09:56.030
RDMA unique will
directly copy the source

00:09:56.031 --> 00:09:58.890
tensor to test
the tensor's memory.

00:09:58.891 --> 00:10:01.640
In the receiver side,
since the RDMA didn't

00:10:01.641 --> 00:10:04.684
have the notification mechanism,

00:10:04.685 --> 00:10:08.090
so the receiver operator
will continuously pulling

00:10:08.091 --> 00:10:11.560
off a megabyte until
the value turned to one,

00:10:11.561 --> 00:10:13.565
which will mean
the whole tensor data

00:10:13.566 --> 00:10:15.390
has already arrived.

00:10:15.391 --> 00:10:20.669
So, it just simply

00:10:20.920 --> 00:10:25.770
passes a reference of tensor
to the downside operators.

00:10:25.771 --> 00:10:26.930
As we can see,

00:10:26.931 --> 00:10:29.975
this communication mechanism
of neural optimizer,

00:10:29.976 --> 00:10:32.170
we can completely remove

00:10:32.171 --> 00:10:34.010
the memory copy overhead in

00:10:34.011 --> 00:10:36.230
the both sender side
and the receiver side,

00:10:36.231 --> 00:10:40.440
and most importantly without
affecting the usability.

00:10:41.970 --> 00:10:45.330
So, this is the
performance evaluation

00:10:45.331 --> 00:10:47.620
for our optimizer,

00:10:47.621 --> 00:10:50.120
the communication mechanism on

00:10:50.121 --> 00:10:51.510
both deep learning frameworks and

00:10:51.511 --> 00:10:53.565
a real training workload.

00:10:53.566 --> 00:10:56.785
So, our experiments are
conducting a cluster with

00:10:56.786 --> 00:10:59.340
eight silvers to evaluate
the performance we

00:10:59.341 --> 00:11:02.340
implement our optimizer
in TensorFlow.

00:11:02.341 --> 00:11:05.055
The left figure shows
the training throughput

00:11:05.056 --> 00:11:08.375
of TensorFlow and
our optimizer version,

00:11:08.376 --> 00:11:12.010
our set of deep learning
benchmarks including RN,

00:11:12.011 --> 00:11:14.575
CN, and the fully-connected work.

00:11:14.576 --> 00:11:18.710
As we can see with
our transparent organization,

00:11:18.711 --> 00:11:21.040
in same hardware environment,

00:11:21.041 --> 00:11:22.980
the performance can
be improved from

00:11:22.981 --> 00:11:26.350
1.8 times to 8.1 times.

00:11:26.351 --> 00:11:28.765
We also demonstrated the benefits

00:11:28.766 --> 00:11:30.375
our end-to-end sequence to

00:11:30.376 --> 00:11:32.895
sequence translation model using

00:11:32.896 --> 00:11:35.805
public data sets which is
showing in the right figure.

00:11:35.806 --> 00:11:37.810
The y-axis is
the perplexity which

00:11:37.811 --> 00:11:40.605
measures the convergence quality.

00:11:40.606 --> 00:11:42.765
The lower, the better.

00:11:42.766 --> 00:11:45.020
The x-axis is the training time.

00:11:45.021 --> 00:11:47.190
As we can see with optimization,

00:11:47.191 --> 00:11:51.050
the current density can be
sped up by two to three times.

00:11:53.540 --> 00:11:56.500
So, after demonstrating
the benefit of

00:11:56.501 --> 00:11:57.980
our global optimizer for

00:11:57.981 --> 00:12:00.130
distributed deep
learning computation,

00:12:00.131 --> 00:12:03.060
now let's look at our case
of local optimizer.

00:12:03.061 --> 00:12:05.405
So, we are mainly talking about

00:12:05.406 --> 00:12:08.415
the kernel fusion for
deep learning on GPU.

00:12:08.416 --> 00:12:11.040
The local optimizer
is merely optimizing

00:12:11.041 --> 00:12:13.545
subgraph unless specific device.

00:12:13.546 --> 00:12:16.075
Currently we mainly
target through GPU.

00:12:16.076 --> 00:12:18.620
So, as I introduced before,

00:12:18.621 --> 00:12:22.640
deep learning framework
often model computation

00:12:22.641 --> 00:12:24.750
as a graph of operators.

00:12:24.751 --> 00:12:27.370
Using graph has
several advantages.

00:12:27.371 --> 00:12:31.410
First it, has a good
expressivity to represent

00:12:31.411 --> 00:12:33.880
arbitrary neural network
structure through just

00:12:33.881 --> 00:12:37.700
composing the graph with
the primitive operators.

00:12:37.701 --> 00:12:40.745
Second, it is flexible
to run the graph on

00:12:40.746 --> 00:12:42.815
multiple devices or
multiple servers

00:12:42.816 --> 00:12:45.810
through just a different graph
partitioning strategies.

00:12:45.811 --> 00:12:48.050
However, this flexibility comes

00:12:48.051 --> 00:12:50.355
with a significant overhead.

00:12:50.356 --> 00:12:53.890
Since the typical
DL model contains

00:12:53.891 --> 00:12:57.495
[inaudible] even more operators,

00:12:57.496 --> 00:12:58.890
scheduling overhead is

00:12:58.891 --> 00:13:02.070
a major bottleneck
for the computation.

00:13:02.071 --> 00:13:05.685
So, to schedule this overhead,

00:13:05.686 --> 00:13:08.080
we are introducing the
overhead like kernel launch

00:13:08.081 --> 00:13:11.610
overhand and the cross operator
communication overhand.

00:13:11.611 --> 00:13:15.685
Also, since the primitive
operator is the two fine-grade,

00:13:15.686 --> 00:13:18.365
it is hard to leverage vendors

00:13:18.366 --> 00:13:22.650
library for a specific
computation workload.

00:13:25.880 --> 00:13:29.760
To demonstrate this, we
compared the execution time of

00:13:29.761 --> 00:13:34.945
a 80-step LSTM model in
TensorFlow and CUDNN.

00:13:34.946 --> 00:13:38.575
CUDNN is a vendor provided
library from Nvidia.

00:13:38.576 --> 00:13:45.785
In TensorFlow, this model is
constructed by about 1,600

00:13:45.786 --> 00:13:49.620
operators and the framework
has to schedule out of

00:13:49.621 --> 00:13:53.580
these operators in this subgraph
during the computation.

00:13:53.581 --> 00:13:55.760
However, in CUDNN,
since it is highly

00:13:55.761 --> 00:13:59.110
optimized for removing
scheduling overhead,

00:13:59.111 --> 00:14:03.230
it can order magnitude
performance improvements

00:14:03.231 --> 00:14:04.915
for the same workload.

00:14:04.916 --> 00:14:06.930
We also tried to manually fuse

00:14:06.931 --> 00:14:11.935
all the kernels into
one CUDNN kernel.

00:14:11.936 --> 00:14:16.370
It shows that we can get
similar performance with CUDNN.

00:14:16.510 --> 00:14:18.680
So, now let's have

00:14:18.681 --> 00:14:22.090
a high-level comparison of
deep learning frameworks and

00:14:22.091 --> 00:14:24.000
the hardware specific
library in terms of

00:14:24.001 --> 00:14:26.840
programming flexibility and
the computation efficiency.

00:14:26.841 --> 00:14:29.060
You see this is

00:14:29.061 --> 00:14:31.965
the general challenge of
encountered DL frameworks.

00:14:31.966 --> 00:14:36.550
First, the deep
learning frameworks

00:14:36.551 --> 00:14:38.070
like TensorFlow, PyTorch,

00:14:38.071 --> 00:14:41.300
and the CNTK, has
designed for user

00:14:41.301 --> 00:14:45.240
to easily program
their deep DL models.

00:14:45.241 --> 00:14:48.230
This is has mostly embrace
the flexibility and

00:14:48.231 --> 00:14:49.930
expressivity through using

00:14:49.931 --> 00:14:52.895
the graph operators
to model computation.

00:14:52.896 --> 00:14:55.375
But they target less about

00:14:55.376 --> 00:15:00.080
the extreme performance
of organizations.

00:15:00.081 --> 00:15:03.910
The other side, the DL
libraries are designed by

00:15:03.911 --> 00:15:05.610
hardware vendors to push

00:15:05.611 --> 00:15:08.710
the extreme performance boundary.

00:15:08.711 --> 00:15:11.460
However, they are
mostly hand-tuned and

00:15:11.461 --> 00:15:14.465
highly optimized for
submissive sub-module,

00:15:14.466 --> 00:15:16.470
it is impossible to handle

00:15:16.471 --> 00:15:19.005
customized or new
network structure,

00:15:19.006 --> 00:15:21.060
which means they have
good efficiency but

00:15:21.061 --> 00:15:23.580
poor flexibility to
spot arbitrary model.

00:15:23.581 --> 00:15:28.760
That's if we have a compiler
that can dynamically

00:15:28.761 --> 00:15:31.540
generates a library like
a code in the right time

00:15:31.541 --> 00:15:33.180
based on input data flow graph

00:15:33.181 --> 00:15:34.560
for deep learning frameworks,

00:15:34.561 --> 00:15:36.290
we can get both
the flexibility of

00:15:36.291 --> 00:15:39.265
the overwork and
the computation efficiency.

00:15:39.266 --> 00:15:41.150
So, under this situation,

00:15:41.151 --> 00:15:44.660
we propose the compiler
stack for optimizing

00:15:44.661 --> 00:15:48.990
deep learning computation
so-called Wolong.

00:15:48.991 --> 00:15:51.000
Wolong compiler takes input as

00:15:51.001 --> 00:15:53.400
a computation graph and it has

00:15:53.401 --> 00:15:59.110
two optimization layers
the graph level optimizer

00:15:59.111 --> 00:16:01.890
and the target specific
data compiler.

00:16:01.891 --> 00:16:03.930
The graph level organizer applies

00:16:03.931 --> 00:16:05.690
a set of target independent

00:16:05.691 --> 00:16:09.745
graph optimization passes
through recovery rewriting.

00:16:09.746 --> 00:16:12.790
So, commonly sets as CSEE,

00:16:12.791 --> 00:16:14.260
constant folding, and so on.

00:16:14.261 --> 00:16:17.980
So, spatial organization in

00:16:17.981 --> 00:16:20.555
the user layer is
operator batching,

00:16:20.556 --> 00:16:22.960
which can automatically
batch same type of

00:16:22.961 --> 00:16:26.470
operators to better leverage
the best efficiency,

00:16:26.471 --> 00:16:29.250
which I will introduce later.

00:16:29.251 --> 00:16:31.110
The second layer is the target

00:16:31.111 --> 00:16:33.325
specific JIT compiler which

00:16:33.326 --> 00:16:37.305
can fuse all the compiler
subgraph into efficient kernel,

00:16:37.306 --> 00:16:40.550
commonly mainly
targeted to GPU device.

00:16:40.551 --> 00:16:44.180
The organization in
this optimizer include

00:16:44.181 --> 00:16:49.465
static shape inference
and type inference,

00:16:49.466 --> 00:16:50.955
and static memory planning,

00:16:50.956 --> 00:16:53.450
and aggressive kernel fusion.

00:16:53.470 --> 00:16:56.620
So, here is the general

00:16:56.621 --> 00:16:58.485
execution flow of
Wolong compiler.

00:16:58.486 --> 00:17:00.660
The graph level
organizer works before

00:17:00.661 --> 00:17:02.235
the graph execution and

00:17:02.236 --> 00:17:05.155
the JIT compiler works
during the execution.

00:17:05.156 --> 00:17:07.460
First, in the graph
level organizer,

00:17:07.461 --> 00:17:09.465
when getting an input graph,

00:17:09.466 --> 00:17:11.530
it first detect some subgraph

00:17:11.531 --> 00:17:14.380
that can apply optimizations.

00:17:14.381 --> 00:17:15.830
For example, if the graph

00:17:15.831 --> 00:17:18.760
contains same type of operators,

00:17:18.761 --> 00:17:22.220
the operator batching patch
will automatically merge

00:17:22.221 --> 00:17:23.520
these operators into

00:17:23.521 --> 00:17:26.495
a larger one through
graph rewritting.

00:17:26.496 --> 00:17:29.380
So, after the graph optimization,

00:17:29.381 --> 00:17:32.670
we start the real execution.

00:17:32.671 --> 00:17:35.705
For a subgraph that is
the first time to be executed,

00:17:35.706 --> 00:17:37.220
our compiler will conduct

00:17:37.221 --> 00:17:41.664
a shape inference and a type
inference for this subgraph,

00:17:41.665 --> 00:17:44.375
as well as a static
memory planning process

00:17:44.376 --> 00:17:47.665
based on the graph
and the GPU features.

00:17:47.666 --> 00:17:50.150
After that, we automatically

00:17:50.151 --> 00:17:52.370
generate a fuse
the kernel code for

00:17:52.371 --> 00:17:55.250
the whole subgraph and
use the JIT compiler to

00:17:55.251 --> 00:17:58.525
compile the code
into a new kernel.

00:17:58.526 --> 00:18:05.960
Finally we replace
the original operators

00:18:05.961 --> 00:18:09.720
in the subgraph in
this new compiled kernel

00:18:09.721 --> 00:18:11.810
and then execute it.

00:18:11.811 --> 00:18:13.800
So, in later iterations,

00:18:13.801 --> 00:18:18.555
if the graph and the input
shapes has never been changed,

00:18:18.556 --> 00:18:20.460
it will hit the cache
and directly

00:18:20.461 --> 00:18:22.665
execute it from
pre-compiled kernel.

00:18:22.666 --> 00:18:24.530
Since we dynamically fused

00:18:24.531 --> 00:18:27.880
all the operators of the
subgraph into a single kernel,

00:18:27.881 --> 00:18:31.140
it can remove all the
scheduling overhead.

00:18:31.141 --> 00:18:34.030
In the following slides I
will introduce a case of

00:18:34.031 --> 00:18:38.995
operator batching and the
details of a JIT compiler.

00:18:38.996 --> 00:18:42.310
So, first, let's look at how

00:18:42.311 --> 00:18:45.575
the operator batching in
graph level organizer works.

00:18:45.576 --> 00:18:50.030
Here we introduce the case of
Mat multiplication merging.

00:18:50.031 --> 00:18:52.230
As we know,
Mat multiplications is

00:18:52.231 --> 00:18:55.145
a very common operator
in DL workloads.

00:18:55.146 --> 00:18:58.275
It has the mathematical
equivalent to lose.

00:18:58.276 --> 00:19:01.530
If two Mat operator shares
one same input tensor,

00:19:01.531 --> 00:19:04.130
we can concatenate
the other inputs to

00:19:04.131 --> 00:19:06.970
merge these two MatMul
as a larger one.

00:19:06.971 --> 00:19:08.850
As the outputs of
the merged MatMul

00:19:08.851 --> 00:19:10.590
is equivalent to
the concatenation of

00:19:10.591 --> 00:19:15.015
the outputs of the two
original MatMul operators.

00:19:15.016 --> 00:19:18.250
Based on this issue,
we can actually

00:19:18.251 --> 00:19:21.555
automatically detects
this pattern in our optimizer

00:19:21.556 --> 00:19:24.830
and rewrite the graphs
through Insert Concat and

00:19:24.831 --> 00:19:29.060
discrete operator
to automatically

00:19:29.061 --> 00:19:30.950
merge the MatMul operators.

00:19:30.951 --> 00:19:32.765
So, after graph rewriting,

00:19:32.766 --> 00:19:35.295
we can do further optimizations.

00:19:35.296 --> 00:19:38.620
So, our tensor placement
optimizer will get

00:19:38.621 --> 00:19:41.580
this information and it will
track this input tensor

00:19:41.581 --> 00:19:44.165
and allocate them physically

00:19:44.166 --> 00:19:46.920
continuously across
the memory so that

00:19:46.921 --> 00:19:50.290
the Concat operator can
overwrite the memory copy during

00:19:50.291 --> 00:19:54.780
its computation as illustrated
in the right figure.

00:19:54.781 --> 00:19:57.865
With such kind of
a graph level rewriting

00:19:57.866 --> 00:20:01.090
optimization and
memory placement optimization,

00:20:01.091 --> 00:20:02.590
we can transparently optimize

00:20:02.591 --> 00:20:05.185
the graph for better performance.

00:20:05.186 --> 00:20:08.035
So, after the graph optimization,

00:20:08.036 --> 00:20:11.330
let's move to
the JIT Compiler part.

00:20:13.460 --> 00:20:16.740
So, in this talk we'll
really focused on how to

00:20:16.741 --> 00:20:20.000
do the kernel fusion
in our JIT compiler.

00:20:20.001 --> 00:20:24.320
Compared with other
optimization frameworks,

00:20:24.321 --> 00:20:27.445
our goal is to conduct
a very aggressive kernel fusion.

00:20:27.446 --> 00:20:30.500
Ideally, we would like to fuse

00:20:30.501 --> 00:20:32.415
the whole subgraph
into a single kernel

00:20:32.416 --> 00:20:35.075
to completely remove the
scheduling overhead.

00:20:35.076 --> 00:20:38.320
First, let's start from
element-wise kind of fusion.

00:20:38.321 --> 00:20:41.420
This type of operators includes

00:20:41.421 --> 00:20:44.910
the binary operators
like the and,

00:20:44.911 --> 00:20:47.835
sub, multiply, divide, and so on,

00:20:47.836 --> 00:20:51.530
and the unitary operators
like sigmoid, and so on.

00:20:51.531 --> 00:20:58.560
For example if we want to
compute a sigmoid of x1+x2,

00:20:58.561 --> 00:21:04.450
the computation graph constructed

00:21:04.451 --> 00:21:05.760
by your framework will contain

00:21:05.761 --> 00:21:09.445
two element operators
and a sigmoid operator,

00:21:09.446 --> 00:21:11.890
and so in the left figure.

00:21:13.340 --> 00:21:15.500
So, the right figure this is

00:21:15.501 --> 00:21:19.610
a corresponding element-wise
data dependency graph.

00:21:19.611 --> 00:21:21.790
As we can see for
element-wise operators,

00:21:21.791 --> 00:21:25.340
there is no
cross-elements dependency

00:21:25.341 --> 00:21:27.320
for this computation.

00:21:27.321 --> 00:21:29.690
So, we can simply assign

00:21:29.691 --> 00:21:32.060
the computation for
each element to

00:21:32.061 --> 00:21:34.450
a single [inaudible] and leverage

00:21:34.451 --> 00:21:37.910
the [inaudible] register memory

00:21:37.911 --> 00:21:41.420
to shares their
intermediate results.

00:21:43.410 --> 00:21:50.540
So, for this workload,

00:21:50.541 --> 00:21:53.285
the generated code look
nicely in the figure.

00:21:53.286 --> 00:21:58.140
It's relatively trivial.

00:21:58.141 --> 00:22:02.490
Since the fusion and device
kernel is really simple.

00:22:02.491 --> 00:22:04.770
So, many other
compiler [inaudible]

00:22:04.771 --> 00:22:07.665
also support this kind of fusion.

00:22:07.666 --> 00:22:10.530
However, fusions doesn't
element-wise operator.

00:22:10.531 --> 00:22:12.140
We think it's not enough

00:22:12.141 --> 00:22:14.520
because the element-wise
operator only occupies

00:22:14.521 --> 00:22:18.900
a very small fraction of
the other operator sets.

00:22:18.901 --> 00:22:20.415
That's our goal is to do

00:22:20.416 --> 00:22:23.300
aggressive kernel fusion to
fuse arbitrary operators.

00:22:23.301 --> 00:22:25.255
To support arbitrary
kernel fusion,

00:22:25.256 --> 00:22:27.290
there are some challenge
we need to address.

00:22:27.291 --> 00:22:29.880
First, unlike
the element-wise operator

00:22:29.881 --> 00:22:32.235
two non element-wise operator

00:22:32.236 --> 00:22:35.250
often has cross data
elements dependencies.

00:22:35.251 --> 00:22:37.200
We have to carefully control

00:22:37.201 --> 00:22:39.355
the dependency for
each convenience thread.

00:22:39.356 --> 00:22:42.600
Second, grade level global
synchronization inquiry is

00:22:42.601 --> 00:22:47.725
a critical permitive forces
kind of workload fusion.

00:22:47.726 --> 00:22:49.950
However, current
GPU architecture has

00:22:49.951 --> 00:22:51.730
fundamental challenge to support

00:22:51.731 --> 00:22:53.625
a general global synchronization.

00:22:53.626 --> 00:22:56.170
Certain some
communication between

00:22:56.171 --> 00:22:59.125
operator has to rely
on the device memory,

00:22:59.126 --> 00:23:01.310
which may increase
the memory usage.

00:23:01.311 --> 00:23:02.750
We have to carefully optimize

00:23:02.751 --> 00:23:05.280
the memory usage and
the buffer allocation.

00:23:05.281 --> 00:23:08.705
To get better understand
let's look at an example for

00:23:08.706 --> 00:23:12.665
fusion to continuous
MatMul operator.

00:23:12.666 --> 00:23:14.270
Since each thread off

00:23:14.271 --> 00:23:16.490
the second MatMul has
to read almost out

00:23:16.491 --> 00:23:21.205
the data element from other
thread of the first MatMul.

00:23:21.206 --> 00:23:24.140
So, a realistic way to do

00:23:24.141 --> 00:23:27.070
the fusion is to store
the results first

00:23:27.071 --> 00:23:30.180
MatMul into device memory
and the second MatMul

00:23:30.181 --> 00:23:33.715
can directly read the inputs
from intermediate buffer.

00:23:33.716 --> 00:23:35.720
So, the right figure
is pseudocode

00:23:35.721 --> 00:23:37.130
for this kind of fusion.

00:23:37.131 --> 00:23:40.700
We can see, we insert
a global barrier

00:23:40.701 --> 00:23:44.740
between the two
MatMul function call.

00:23:44.741 --> 00:23:49.150
So however, the fact
is in GPU architecture

00:23:49.151 --> 00:23:53.960
this code is really
challenging to run.

00:23:53.961 --> 00:23:56.500
So, to understand
why the challenge

00:23:56.501 --> 00:23:58.270
is happening for
GPU architecture.

00:23:58.271 --> 00:24:02.260
So, to first we need to
understand the- let's look

00:24:02.261 --> 00:24:04.370
at how the current DL

00:24:04.371 --> 00:24:07.475
frameworks schedule
dataflow graph in GPU.

00:24:07.476 --> 00:24:09.740
So, typically the
DL framework uses

00:24:09.741 --> 00:24:11.850
CPU to schedule operators

00:24:11.851 --> 00:24:13.900
and the GPU is responsible

00:24:13.901 --> 00:24:16.895
for executing
each operators kernel.

00:24:16.896 --> 00:24:20.720
So, the GPU often consists of
several stream processors,

00:24:20.721 --> 00:24:26.000
which can concurrently execute
block task of GPU kernel.

00:24:26.001 --> 00:24:29.160
Let's say there is a graph
with only two operators,

00:24:29.161 --> 00:24:30.725
MatMul and the convolution.

00:24:30.726 --> 00:24:34.305
The convolution either read
the result of the MatMul.

00:24:34.306 --> 00:24:36.400
To run this graph in GPU,

00:24:36.401 --> 00:24:37.930
DL framework has to schedule

00:24:37.931 --> 00:24:40.455
this two operators one by one.

00:24:40.456 --> 00:24:44.310
For each operator, we need
to explicitly split it as

00:24:44.311 --> 00:24:48.935
multiple block tasks
according to the data size,

00:24:48.936 --> 00:24:54.790
and schedule them on to
multiple GPU stream processors.

00:24:54.791 --> 00:24:57.300
Due to the limited number
of stream processors,

00:24:57.301 --> 00:24:59.860
the blocks has to be
implicitly execute

00:24:59.861 --> 00:25:02.740
in several batches like this.

00:25:02.741 --> 00:25:04.260
So then, the second operator is

00:25:04.261 --> 00:25:06.800
scheduled in a similar way.

00:25:07.400 --> 00:25:09.555
As I mentioned before,

00:25:09.556 --> 00:25:12.315
if the graph contains
a lot of operators,

00:25:12.316 --> 00:25:13.910
the kernel launching and

00:25:13.911 --> 00:25:17.740
the operator scheduler will
become significant overhead.

00:25:17.741 --> 00:25:21.245
So, a straightforward way
to remove this overhead is

00:25:21.246 --> 00:25:22.705
fusing all the operators of

00:25:22.706 --> 00:25:24.740
several graphs into
a single GPU kernel.

00:25:24.741 --> 00:25:26.440
For example, if you want to fuse

00:25:26.441 --> 00:25:27.930
the MatMul and
the convolution within

00:25:27.931 --> 00:25:32.725
a single kernel so we can
compose them as two-stage.

00:25:32.726 --> 00:25:34.220
The first stage do the MatMul and

00:25:34.221 --> 00:25:37.030
the second stage do
the convolution.

00:25:37.031 --> 00:25:39.579
So consequently,

00:25:39.580 --> 00:25:43.460
the other block paths
also include a two-stage.

00:25:43.461 --> 00:25:47.620
However, since this two kernel
has cross-validated data

00:25:47.621 --> 00:25:49.580
dependencies we have to insert

00:25:49.581 --> 00:25:52.650
a barrier between
the two-stages in the kernel.

00:25:52.651 --> 00:25:56.405
So, for the outer
blocks it also has

00:25:56.406 --> 00:26:01.640
the- so each block has to
enter a global barrier.

00:26:01.641 --> 00:26:03.910
However, as I
mentioned it before,

00:26:03.911 --> 00:26:06.265
due to a limited SM resources,

00:26:06.266 --> 00:26:08.070
only the first several blocks of

00:26:08.071 --> 00:26:10.365
the kernel were
real as scheduled.

00:26:10.366 --> 00:26:13.980
They will be blocked in
waiting for barrier.

00:26:13.981 --> 00:26:17.180
So, as the GPU has

00:26:17.181 --> 00:26:18.770
no block contacts switch

00:26:18.771 --> 00:26:21.320
the later several blocks
will never be scheduled.

00:26:21.321 --> 00:26:24.370
So, this kernel will
fail to execute.

00:26:24.620 --> 00:26:27.785
So, to address these issues
we proposed to use

00:26:27.786 --> 00:26:30.490
the persistent threads and
the virtual block mechanism.

00:26:30.491 --> 00:26:34.065
To fuse the same two operators
into a single kernel,

00:26:34.066 --> 00:26:35.750
refers to the limited number of

00:26:35.751 --> 00:26:38.720
physical blocks off
the fused kernel according to

00:26:38.721 --> 00:26:40.810
the available resources so that

00:26:40.811 --> 00:26:45.635
all the blocks can be
executed concurrently.

00:26:45.636 --> 00:26:48.960
Then, we treat the original
blocks of each operator as

00:26:48.961 --> 00:26:51.160
a virtual block and assign them

00:26:51.161 --> 00:26:53.985
to physical blocks in
a round-robin way.

00:26:53.986 --> 00:26:56.280
We implemented this
through remapping

00:26:56.281 --> 00:26:59.660
their block ID in the
code generation time.

00:27:00.410 --> 00:27:02.680
Then we are free to insert

00:27:02.681 --> 00:27:04.075
a barrier between the two-stage

00:27:04.076 --> 00:27:06.330
of virtual blocks since

00:27:06.331 --> 00:27:08.625
all the physical block
already active.

00:27:08.626 --> 00:27:10.560
With this mechanism we can fuse

00:27:10.561 --> 00:27:12.285
all the kernel into a single GPU

00:27:12.286 --> 00:27:16.950
as long as we can generate
each operators self code.

00:27:19.370 --> 00:27:22.810
So, till now to fuse
multiple operators we are

00:27:22.811 --> 00:27:27.240
actually generating
a statistic sequence

00:27:27.241 --> 00:27:29.130
of code for each operator.

00:27:29.131 --> 00:27:31.260
For example, for
the left dataflow graph

00:27:31.261 --> 00:27:32.784
with three operators,

00:27:32.785 --> 00:27:35.180
they will be
sequentially assigned to

00:27:35.181 --> 00:27:38.845
physical blocks like this.

00:27:38.846 --> 00:27:40.500
However, since the sigmoid,

00:27:40.501 --> 00:27:43.575
let's say the graph,

00:27:43.576 --> 00:27:47.190
since the sigmoid operator
and the end operator are

00:27:47.191 --> 00:27:51.095
small operators and they
have no data dependencies,

00:27:51.096 --> 00:27:53.950
if we use a counter
DL framework they

00:27:53.951 --> 00:28:01.070
can leverage
data parallelization.

00:28:01.071 --> 00:28:04.690
So, in our fusion
frameworks there

00:28:04.691 --> 00:28:07.980
is some underutilization
for GPU resources.

00:28:07.981 --> 00:28:10.045
To further push resource usage

00:28:10.046 --> 00:28:12.065
we support the kernel
packing mechanism,

00:28:12.066 --> 00:28:14.040
which you can pack
the small kernels into

00:28:14.041 --> 00:28:18.370
a more compact way during
the virtual block assignment.

00:28:18.371 --> 00:28:20.410
So, we can pack

00:28:20.411 --> 00:28:26.875
the end operator into
the sigmoid operators batch.

00:28:26.876 --> 00:28:29.460
In this way we can exploit
the graph level paralyze

00:28:29.461 --> 00:28:34.450
and removes kernel launch
overhead in the same time.

00:28:35.780 --> 00:28:39.395
Okay. So, this is a way of how to

00:28:39.396 --> 00:28:43.205
generate to fuse
the kernel coding over compiler.

00:28:43.206 --> 00:28:47.400
Basically, first for each
operator we need to register

00:28:47.401 --> 00:28:49.590
its kernel source
code and some other

00:28:49.591 --> 00:28:52.090
meta information to
our code generator.

00:28:52.091 --> 00:28:55.490
We can only fuse the operator
that has been registered.

00:28:55.491 --> 00:28:58.210
For arbitrary graph to be fused,

00:28:58.211 --> 00:29:00.230
we first conduct
shipping inference

00:29:00.231 --> 00:29:01.700
and the memory place planning

00:29:01.701 --> 00:29:03.280
paths and then generate

00:29:03.281 --> 00:29:05.590
the function cause
for each operators.

00:29:05.591 --> 00:29:08.015
For example, for
this graph we can

00:29:08.016 --> 00:29:10.480
first generate
the sigmoid and then

00:29:10.481 --> 00:29:13.405
the MatMul and the

00:29:13.406 --> 00:29:18.120
final is for the real
operators in this way.

00:29:18.121 --> 00:29:21.460
So, during the process we
will automatically insert

00:29:21.461 --> 00:29:25.200
a barrier we need it and
the packing kernel if possible.

00:29:25.201 --> 00:29:27.880
Currently, with
all these spots a lot of

00:29:27.881 --> 00:29:31.620
operators we are still in
the more into the framework.

00:29:31.621 --> 00:29:33.745
So, now let's look at our

00:29:33.746 --> 00:29:36.190
memory performance scale
of a kernel fusion.

00:29:36.191 --> 00:29:38.650
For the RNN inference
benchmark with

00:29:38.651 --> 00:29:42.585
80-steps LSTM as we show
you at the beginning.

00:29:42.586 --> 00:29:44.300
Currently, our compiler can

00:29:44.301 --> 00:29:46.330
automatically fuse
and then compile

00:29:46.331 --> 00:29:48.055
the whole graph with

00:29:48.056 --> 00:29:51.660
1,600 operators into
a single GPU kernel.

00:29:51.661 --> 00:29:53.560
The right figure demonstrates

00:29:53.561 --> 00:29:56.310
the performance comparison
with original TensorFlow.

00:29:56.311 --> 00:30:00.045
The bar with the title
OpBatch means we only

00:30:00.046 --> 00:30:01.475
apply the operator batching

00:30:01.476 --> 00:30:05.140
optimizations in the graph
layer level optimizations.

00:30:05.141 --> 00:30:09.335
The fusion bar is the final
result of the kernel fusion.

00:30:09.336 --> 00:30:11.740
We can see with our
compiler we can get

00:30:11.741 --> 00:30:15.295
about 10x speedup
for this workload.

00:30:15.296 --> 00:30:18.690
So, in conclusion we think
compiler infrastructure

00:30:18.691 --> 00:30:22.290
is critical for both
cloud and the edge AI

00:30:22.291 --> 00:30:24.240
because they can
automatically and

00:30:24.241 --> 00:30:27.070
transparently optimize both
the distributed training and

00:30:27.071 --> 00:30:34.090
the local inference
on different devices.

00:30:34.091 --> 00:30:36.890
I also think our system
innovation is the key to bridge

00:30:36.891 --> 00:30:40.195
the application and
diverse hardware.

00:30:40.196 --> 00:30:45.560
Since the current DFL already
abstract a common IR,

00:30:45.561 --> 00:30:47.320
which means the dataflow graph

00:30:47.321 --> 00:30:49.540
IR and we can leverage this

00:30:49.541 --> 00:30:51.420
to co-design software and

00:30:51.421 --> 00:30:53.665
hardware for extreme efficiency.

00:30:53.666 --> 00:30:55.840
We also have developed

00:30:55.841 --> 00:30:57.820
a Wolong prototype to

00:30:57.821 --> 00:31:00.045
demonstrate the
initial improvements.

00:31:00.046 --> 00:31:04.170
It's up to eight times
speed up for training

00:31:04.171 --> 00:31:08.820
and 10x speedup on the
inference workload. Thank you.

00:31:08.821 --> 00:31:10.470
That's my talk.

